.TH TERMNAV 1 "August 2026" "termnav" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
termnav \- route terminal navigation, focus, links, and editor opens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termnav navigate
.RI [ options ] " ACTION DIRECTION"
.br
.B termnav relay send
.I ACTION DIRECTION
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B termnav relay serve --socket
.I PATH
.RI [ --owner-fd " FD" ]
.br
.B termnav relay commit
.I options
.br
.B termnav relay sweep
.br
.B termnav ssh
.RI [ SSH-ARG ...]
.br
.B termnav link-host
.br
.B termnav tmux context
.I options
.br
.B termnav tmux focus
.I claim|release|expire|watch|stop|sync options
.br
.B termnav tmux attention
.I options
.br
.B termnav tmux follow-click
.RI [ --stdin | " FIELD ..." ]
.br
.B termnav nvim open cli
.I FILE
.RI [ CWD ]
.br
.B termnav nvim open link|tmux-link
.RI [ TARGET " [" CWD " [" SOURCE " [" CONTEXT " [" ROUTE-KIND " [" ROUTE-SCOPE " [" PANE "]]]]]]]" ]
.br
.B termnav nvim ssh-open
.I HOST TARGET
.br
.B termnav open-url
.RI [ --tty " PATH" ] " URL"
.br
.B termnav vscode focus claim|release
.I SOURCE CYCLE SEQUENCE OBSERVED
.br
.B termnav eza
.RI [ EZA-ARG ...]
.br
.B termnav asset-path
.I RELATIVE-PATH
.br
.B termnav version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B termnav
is one native command for navigation across Neovim, tmux, SSH, WezTerm, and
VS Code terminal boundaries. Integrations should invoke the structured
subcommands instead of reproducing routing policy.
.SH COMMAND SYNTAX
.TP
.B navigate
ACTION is
.BR pane-select " with " left|down|up|right ,
.BR pane-move " with " left|down|up|right ,
.BR tab-select " with " previous|next ,
or
.BR tab-move " with " left|right .
Options are
.BR --parent ,
.BR --client-pid ,
.BR --client-tty ,
.BR --client-created ,
.BR --client-termtype ,
.BR --source-socket ,
.BR --source-pane ,
.BR --source-session ,
.BR --emit-continuation ,
.BR --continuation ,
and
.BR "--tty PATH" ,
which names the terminal for a request that reaches it without a tmux client
in place of the controlling terminal; the Neovim adapter passes its editor's
stderr terminal there, because Neovim's TUI runs the editor in a session
without one.
.TP
.B relay send
Uses pane-select, tab-select, and tab-move with their navigation directions and
optionally the paired
.B --client-pid PID --client-tty TTY
identity. Pane-move is host-local and is rejected rather than sent over a
relay.
.TP
.B relay commit
Requires
.BR --tmux-socket ,
.BR --client-tty ,
.BR --client-pid ,
and
.BR --client-created ;
it accepts optional
.B --passthrough-decrqm
and
.BR --pane .
.TP
.B tmux context
Requires
.B --tty TTY --client-termname TERM
and accepts
.BR --control-mode " 0|1" .
.TP
.B tmux focus
.B claim
requires parent tmux, parent pane, token, and lease options;
.B release
requires parent and token;
.B expire
requires parent;
.B watch,
.B stop,
and
.B sync
require the tmux socket and either the client name or the client PID and tty.
Notification hooks such as
.B client-focus-in
pass the name from
.BR #{hook_client} ,
because their
.B #{client_pid}
and
.B #{client_tty}
describe the session's most recently active client rather than the client that
raised the event; a named client that is gone or in control mode is ignored.
tmux 3.2 has no
.BR #{hook_client} ,
so the example falls back to the exact identity there.
Watch also accepts lease and interval options. Command-group help prints the
exact option names.
.TP
.B tmux attention
Requires
.B --tmux-socket SOCKET --client-name NAME --client-pid PID --client-created EPOCH
and
.BR "--working N --pending N --failed N" .
Counts are nonnegative and bounded at 100000; failed cannot exceed pending.
The exact client identity and foreground tty ownership are verified. Status 0
means the parent accepted the snapshot or Termnav published to the exact direct
terminal. Legacy status 3 requires an updated Termnav; callers must not perform
their own tty writes. Status 1 is an error; invalid arguments
return 2. Existing local tmux ancestry and SSH relays are reused.
Child contributions expire after 15 seconds on the receiving host. Direct
terminals receive the TERM_PANE_STATE OSC user variable. Snapshots are advisory:
overlapping deliveries may briefly arrive out of order before reconciliation.
Terminal consumers must expire them using local receipt time.
Direct attention writes use a fresh nonblocking descriptor, verify its device
and inode against the captured character terminal, and revalidate foreground
ownership before writing. Backpressure is bounded to 100 milliseconds; partial
frames fail without replay. CAN at each immediate parser boundary cancels an
earlier incomplete attention escape. This affects output parsing only.
Inherited descriptor flags and navigation commit writes are unchanged.
Terminal reopen failures decline publication rather than use blocking output.
.TP
.B tmux follow-click
Accepts at most eleven positional fields in this order: HYPERLINK, WORD, LINE,
X, CWD, TTY, PANE, Y, TOP, LEFT, SOCKET. With
.BR --stdin ,
the same fields are newline-delimited; input is limited to eleven lines and 64
KiB. A URL target is delivered as by
.BR open-url ,
starting from the client named by TTY; a control-mode or VS Code (xterm.js)
client declines. A declined or failed request runs
.B $BROWSER
with the URL; when nothing opens it, the clicking client shows a message.
X and Y are zero-based window cell coordinates. TOP and LEFT identify the
clicked pane's offsets; SOCKET identifies its exact tmux server. File parsing
uses Unicode grapheme cell widths, preserves genuine a/ and b/ directories,
and retains colons before a numeric line/column suffix.
.TP
.B open-url
Accepts exactly one
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
or
.B mailto
URL and asks the outer terminal to open it on the machine that runs the
terminal, through the
.B TERMNAV_OPEN_URL
request. Other schemes and control characters are invalid syntax. Inside tmux
the request is written to the most recently active client of the caller's
session, preferring clients that show the caller's window and skipping
control-mode and VS Code (xterm.js) clients; otherwise it is written to
.B --tty PATH
when given, or to the controlling terminal. The option serves callers without
a controlling terminal, such as Neovim's TUI server, and is ignored inside tmux.
.IP
The request is written only when its destination is known to be WezTerm, the
only terminal that acts on it. A tmux client is identified by the XTVERSION
reply tmux records as
.BR client_termtype ,
or on tmux releases without one by its
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ,
.BR WEZTERM_PANE ,
or terminal name; a plain terminal by
.B TERM_PROGRAM=WezTerm
(which decides whenever set),
.BR WEZTERM_PANE ,
or
.BR TERM=wezterm .
A client running inside another local tmux is followed to that tmux's client
for the hosting pane, and the request is written there directly; ancestry whose
pane does not own the client's tty is not followed. A tmux layer that cannot be
inspected from this host, such as local tmux behind an SSH session, or one whose
tmux reply proves it is tmux but whose pane cannot be found, receives one
passthrough frame and is trusted; that requires
.B allow-passthrough
on it. GNU screen, recognized by
.BR STY ,
cannot forward the frame and declines. Anything else, including an SSH session
from WezTerm that forwards none of those variables, declines with status 3
before writing. No eligible client or a missing terminal is an operational
failure (1); standard output is never used. Success means the request was
written, not that the terminal acted on it. Callers should fall back to their
own opener on any nonzero status.
.SH SSH RELAY
Interactive SSH sessions use a remote Unix socket below
.B /tmp
by default. A host-specific OpenSSH configuration can select an existing,
writable directory instead:
.PP
.nf
Host phone
    SetEnv TERMNAV_REMOTE_SOCKET_DIR=/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/tmp
.fi
.PP
Termnav reads this hint from its existing
.B ssh -G
query, without another connection, remote command, or environment expansion.
The directory must be absolute and contain only ASCII letters, digits,
slashes, dots, underscores, and hyphens; dot and dot-dot path components are
invalid. Quotes used by OpenSSH configuration are removed by OpenSSH; literal
quotes, spaces, colons, shell expressions, and percent-token expansion in the
resulting directory are unsupported. The complete generated socket path must
fit 103 bytes (currently at most 59 directory bytes excluding trailing
slashes). Repeated hints must agree exactly. Invalid or conflicting hints
delegate the original SSH invocation unchanged, without a Termnav relay.
The directory is not created or remotely probed; OpenSSH reports bind failures.
.PP
The hint does not require remote
.BR AcceptEnv .
The generated
.B TERMNAV_PARENT_RELAY
still requires it, alongside enabled Unix-socket forwarding. The forward,
per-session environment, and local existing-master cleanup use the same path.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERMNAV_ASSET_ROOT
Development/staging override for the installed provider root used by
.BR asset-path .
.TP
.B TERMNAV_PARENT_RELAY
Connection-scoped Unix relay inherited through an SSH session.
.TP
.B TERMNAV_REMOTE_TOOL_PATH
Colon-separated remote command directories appended after inherited PATH.
The default is
$HOME/.local/bin:$HOME/.local/share/mise/shims:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin.
The invoking Termnav embeds this policy in the existing SSH command, so it does
not depend on sshd AcceptEnv.
.TP
.B TERMNAV_VSCODE_FALLBACK_BACKEND=mcp
Enable the MCP fallback. It requires an absolute
.B TERMNAV_VSCODE_MCP_TOKEN_FILE
and accepts optional ADDRESS, PATH, METHOD, TOOL, PREVIOUS_COMMAND, and
NEXT_COMMAND variables with the
.B TERMNAV_VSCODE_MCP_
prefix. The endpoint must remain loopback.
.TP
.B TERMNAV_SSH_CONTROL_HOSTS
Comma-separated allowlist for
.BR "nvim ssh-open" .
.TP
.B TERMNAV_TMUX_FOLLOW_EXTENSION_DIR
Override the directory of optional Ctrl-click token detector scripts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Handled or successful.
.TP
.B 1
Operational failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid syntax.
.TP
.B 3
Valid navigation or relay-send request declined at this boundary, or an
.B open-url
request whose destination is not known to be WezTerm.
.TP
.B 10, 11, 12, 13
.B nvim ssh-open
uses these for unavailable connection configuration, invalid host, SSH failure,
and disallowed host. VS Code focus uses 10 when its adapter is unavailable.
SSH and eza otherwise preserve child status.
.SH PANE MOVEMENT
.B termnav navigate pane-move DIRECTION
swaps the exact active tmux source pane with its directional neighbor and keeps
focus on the moved pane. It may continue through any number of same-host parent
tmux scopes. It declines without mutation at an edge, in a one-pane or zoomed
window, when client ownership is stale or ambiguous, or before an SSH, WezTerm,
VS Code, or terminal boundary.
.PP
The Neovim navigation provider exposes
.BR pane_move(direction) .
With mappings enabled,
.B navigation.setup()
owns M-H, M-J, M-K, and M-L in normal and terminal modes. Local editor movement
starts no process; a tmux movement starts one Termnav process and one guarded
mutation command per attempted local scope. Bounded client inspection and
revalidation queries run only when session identity or outward traversal needs
them. Consumers should invoke the current-scope command without
.B --parent
unless they have already performed and declined that scope's operation.
.SH WEZTERM PROTOCOL
.B routes.setup()
registers the open-uri router and handlers for
.BR TERMNAV_TAB_SELECT ,
.BR TERMNAV_TAB_MOVE ,
and
.BR TERMNAV_OPEN_URL .
.B termnav open-url
is the public publisher for TERMNAV_OPEN_URL; integrations should invoke it
instead of constructing the escape. Because any program whose output reaches
the terminal can set the variable, the handler applies the same scheme and
control-character policy itself and ignores, with a warning that omits the
value, anything the publisher would reject.
Pane metadata uses IS_NVIM, NVIM_OPEN_SOCKET, NVIM_LINK_CWD,
NVIM_REMOTE_LINK_HOST, NVIM_REMOTE_CWD, NVIM_REMOTE_TMUX, and TERMNAV_TMUX.
There are no DOT_* aliases.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/termnav-UID
Owner-only relay, navigation, and focus coordination state.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/nvim-tmux-open
Neovim socket registry shared with the editor integration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR nvim (1)
